# elf module

The `elf` module parses ELF object files and executables. It supports 32-bit and 64-bit ELF.

## Constants

- `MAGIC`: `0x7F`, `E`, `L`, `F`.

## Types

### `Class`

ELF class.

Values: `elf32`, `elf64`.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `FileType`

ELF file type.

Values include `none`, `rel`, `exec`, `dyn`, `core`.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `Machine`

ELF machine type.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SectionType`

ELF section type.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SymbolBinding`

Values: `local`, `global`, `weak`.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SymbolType`

Values: `none`, `object`, `func`, `section`, `file`, and others.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SymbolVisibility`

Values: `default`, `internal`, `hidden`, `protected`.

- `name(self)`: return a readable name.

### `SpecialSectionIndex`

Special section indices.

Constants: `UNDEF = 0`, `LORESERVE = 0xff00`, `ABS = 0xfff1`, `COMMON = 0xfff2`, `XINDEX = 0xffff`.

### `Symbol`

ELF symbol table entry.

Methods:

- `isUndefined(self)`: true for undefined symbols.
- `isAbsolute(self)`: true for absolute symbols.
- `getName(self, strtab_bytes)`: read the symbol name.

### `Relocation`

ELF relocation entry.

- `typeName(self, machine)`: return a readable type name.

### `SectionHeader`

ELF section header.

Methods:

- `isAlloc(self)`: true for allocatable sections.
- `isWritable(self)`: true for writable sections.
- `isExecutable(self)`: true for executable sections.
- `getData(self, file_bytes)`: return section bytes.
- `getName(self, shstrtab_data)`: read the section name.
- `getSymbolCount(self, class)`: count symbols in the section.
- `parseSymbol(self, bytes, class, endian, index)`: parse one symbol.
- `parseAllSymbols(self, allocator, bytes, class, endian)`: parse all symbols.
- `getRelocationCount(self, class)`: count relocations.
- `parseRelocation(self, bytes, class, endian, index)`: parse one relocation.
- `parseAllRelocations(self, allocator, bytes, class, endian)`: parse all relocations.

### `Header`

Parsed ELF header.

Methods:

- `parse(bytes)`: parse the ELF header.
- `parseSectionHeader(self, bytes, index)`: parse one section header.
- `parseAllSectionHeaders(self, allocator, bytes)`: parse all section headers.
- `getSectionStringTable(self, bytes)`: return the section name string table.

### `flags`

ELF section flag constants.

Constants include `WRITE`, `ALLOC`, `EXECINSTR`, `MERGE`, `STRINGS`, `INFO_LINK`, `LINK_ORDER`, `OS_NONCONFORMING`, `GROUP`, and `TLS`.

## Error sets

- `HeaderError`
- `SectionError`
- `SymbolError`
- `RelocationError`

## Example

```zig
const elf_hdr = try sibl.elf.Header.parse(mapped.data);
const shstrtab = try elf_hdr.getSectionStringTable(mapped.data);
const sections = try elf_hdr.parseAllSectionHeaders(allocator, mapped.data);

for (sections, 0..) |sh, idx| {
    if (sh.sh_type == .null) continue;
    const name = try sh.getName(shstrtab);
    std.debug.print("[{d}] {s}\n", .{ idx, name });
}
```

## Notes

- Use `elf_hdr.class` when parsing symbols and relocations.
- Section names come from the section header string table.
- Relocation sections point to their symbol table through `sh.link`.
- The parser keeps zero-copy slices into the mapped file.
